===persian===

گفتی به من تکیه کن
افرا آسمان را مهربان‌تر می‌کند
گفتم
تو ریشه در خاک نداری...
برگ‌ها مسافران فضولند...
برگ‌ها را سرما و گرما می‌سوزاند
اما ریشه‌ها را نه...
کاش از دیار دور... بر می‌گشتی...
تهران، بهار ۱۳۸۴

===machine===

You said, lean on me —
the maple makes the sky kinder.
I said:
you have no root in the soil...
Leaves are meddling travellers...
Cold and heat burn the leaves
but not the roots...
If only you would come back... from that far country...
Tehran, spring 1384

===lantern===

You said: lean on me,
the maple makes the sky kinder.
I said:
you have no root in the soil.
Leaves are meddling travellers.
Cold and heat burn the leaves
but they do not burn the roots.
If only you would come back from that far country...

Tehran, spring 1384

===translation===

===footnotes===

Fourteen years before this, answering a magazine that had asked him how the war decade
treated him, Jafari wrote: *cold and heat burn the leaves and the branches, but not the roots.* He
says it again here, almost word for word, to somebody who has left the country — the same sentence
used first to explain how he survived and then to argue that leaving is a kind of not having
survived.
